::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 GP Q01::Topic: classroom discussion. Which sentence gives the clearest oral presentation perspective? {
~A. Classroom discussion is the topic.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and does not give a focused perspective.
=B. Classroom discussion helps students understand texts because they hear and explain different interpretations.#Correct. This gives a clear point of view and explains the focus.
~C. Classrooms have discussion sometimes.#Incorrect. This is a vague detail, not a perspective.
~D. I will talk about discussion for a while.#Incorrect. This names the activity but does not state the speaker's point.
}

::MLA.ENG1.C.01 U06 L03 GP Q02::Perspective: Weekly planning helps students manage long-term assignments. Which detail best supports the perspective? {
~A. Some planners have spiral binding.#Incorrect. This detail is about a planner's design, not how planning helps students manage assignments.
~B. Many students carry backpacks.#Incorrect. This may be school-related, but it does not support the planning perspective.
~C. Long-term assignments can be divided into smaller tasks across several days.#Correct. This detail supports the perspective by showing how planning helps.
~D. Students sometimes talk in class.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to weekly planning and assignment management.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.08 U06 L03 GP Q03::Perspective: Students should use study groups before quizzes. Opposing view: Some students learn better alone. Which response keeps coherent focus? {
~A. Studying alone is boring, and school lunches should change.#Incorrect. This dismisses the opposing view and shifts to an unrelated topic.
~B. Some students do learn well alone, but study groups can still help students hear explanations they may not create by themselves.#Correct. This acknowledges the opposing view and returns to the main perspective.
~C. Study groups are always perfect for every person.#Incorrect. This is too absolute and does not fairly handle the opposing view.
~D. Quizzes are sometimes difficult.#Incorrect. This is related to school but does not respond to the opposing view.
}

::MLA.ENG1.V.01 U06 L03 GP Q04::Which phrase uses academic vocabulary best in a focused oral presentation? {
=A. coherent presentation focus#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary connected to the lesson skill.
~B. stuff I want to say#Incorrect. This phrase is informal and vague.
~C. a bunch of school things#Incorrect. This wording is too general for academic speaking.
~D. talking about whatever#Incorrect. This phrase shows lack of focus and inappropriate vocabulary.
}

::MLA.ENG1.R.07 U06 L03 GP Q05::A speaker chooses emotional examples to persuade classmates to volunteer. What rhetorical choice is the speaker using? {
~A. Removing the presentation's purpose#Incorrect. The speaker is not removing purpose; the speaker is choosing examples to achieve it.
~B. Changing the audience to adults#Incorrect. The audience remains classmates.
~C. Avoiding perspective#Incorrect. Persuasive examples usually support a perspective.
=D. Using examples to appeal to the audience's feelings#Correct. Emotional examples are a rhetorical choice used to affect the audience.
}
